<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</h1><p class="st-meta">1995 CE – present · capital Addis Ababa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Present-day Ethiopia, a federation of ethnically defined regions and Africa's second most populous country, shaped by rapid growth and civil conflict.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The constitution adopted in December 1994 replaced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transitional-government-of-ethiopia">Transitional Government of Ethiopia</a>, and after elections the Federal Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> was proclaimed in August 1995. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meles-zenawi">Meles Zenawi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> became prime minister, holding power until his death in 2012.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution created a federation of ethnically based regional states, each with the right to self-determination. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> achieved rapid economic growth through state-led investment in roads, dams and industry, but the party dominated politics and suppressed opposition. A war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> from 1998 to 2000 and the disputed elections of 2005 marked its early years.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Protests brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abiy-ahmed">Abiy Ahmed</a> to power in 2018; he made peace with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2019. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a> war of 2020 to 2022, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> agreement, killed hundreds of thousands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> remains a federal republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by User:SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1995 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Addis Ababa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meles Zenawi, Hailemariam Desalegn, Abiy Ahmed</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Islam, Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amharic, Oromo, Tigrinya, Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ethiopia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia" aria-label="Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5750 1840 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6070 2096L6070 2096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6070 2096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 2096h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 2096h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Addis Ababa. <a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
